.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt CAP_PROCESS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cap_process
.Nd manage the calling process's capability discovery context and attribution
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/cap_process.h
.Ft int
.Fn cap_process "int op" "int fd" "uid_t uid" "void *data"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The discovery context is a kernel-held reference to a channel already owned
by the caller.
It survives descriptor cleanup and exec and is inherited by
children.
Replacing or clearing it affects only the calling process and future
children, not its parent, siblings, or existing children.
.Pp
The following operations are supported:
.Bl -tag -width MAC_CAP_PROCESS_ORIGIN_EXPORT
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_SET
Retain channel descriptor
.Fa fd
with the informational UID label
.Fa uid .
The label does not authenticate a user or grant authority.
The caller retains ownership of the descriptor.
Its Capsicum rights are copied.
Descriptors whose fork or exec propagation has been locked or restricted to
one use are rejected.
Installing a reference does not grant new authority.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_GET
Return an owned close-on-exec descriptor for the current discovery context.
Its rights are preserved and descriptor transfer is disabled.
The calling process's prison must match the installed context.
A UNIX UID change does not replace or invalidate the discovery route.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_CLEAR
Release the calling process's context.
Already returned descriptors and
contexts held by other processes remain valid.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_INFO
Copy a
.Vt struct mac_cap_process_info
into
.Fa data .
The structure reports this process's lifetime identity, responsible identity,
context generation, process IDs, informational UID label and whether a
context exists.
The same prison check as GET applies when a context exists.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_ORIGIN_EXPORT
Return an owned close-on-exec descriptor containing this process's immutable
lifetime identity and PID.
The token remains valid after the process exits.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_ORIGIN_SET
Associate the calling process with the origin token in
.Fa fd .
The token must originate in the same prison.
The association is inherited by
children and survives exec.
It grants no service authority and does not change
coalition membership or the caller's own identity.
.El
.Pp
.Fa data
must be NULL except for INFO.
Unused descriptor and UID arguments should be
-1 and zero respectively.
.Pp
A parent selects a different bootstrap for a child by forking, installing the
child's context in the child, then executing its program.
The discovery context supplies a route, not software attributes or service
authority.
Ordinary authentication and UNIX credential changes do not require a new
discovery context.
Service admission uses the requesting software's protected identity and
attributes; installing a route does not create or replace that identity.
The kernel-held reference does not reconnect to a terminated peer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
GET and ORIGIN_EXPORT return a descriptor on success.
Other operations return
zero.
On failure, -1 is returned and errno is set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENOTCAPABLE
.It Bq Er ENOENT
GET was requested without an installed context.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The discovery context or origin token belongs to another prison.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
Installing the descriptor would bypass its fork or exec restrictions, or an
operation exceeds its Capsicum rights.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The operation, object type, or data argument is invalid.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The supplied descriptor is invalid.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The calling process cannot allocate another descriptor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The INFO output address is invalid.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr closefrom 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr libservice 3
